From: Manuel de Brito Fontes <aledbf@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 01:26:15 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: Save a device's state at add only where error recovery
 can use it

Commit a2f1e22390ac ("PCI/ERR: Ensure error recoverability at all
times") saves every device's configuration space in
pci_bus_add_device(), so that a driver's error handlers can restore it
after an error has already lost it, and so that pci_restore_state()
no longer needs to refuse a state that was never saved. The save reads
the first 64 bytes of configuration space a dword at a time, and the
capability state pci_save_state() keeps.

A kernel built without anything that calls a driver's error handlers
never restores that state. Error recovery through pcie_do_recovery()
needs CONFIG_PCIEAER, which DPC and EDR depend on, and the other
callers of the handlers are EEH, s390's zPCI and Xen's PCI frontend.
Every other path that loses the state saves it first: runtime and
system suspend in pci-driver.c, hibernation's freeze, the reset
methods through pci_dev_save_and_disable() and the bus and slot resets
through pci_bus_save_and_disable_locked(). The one other restore of a
state nobody saved is pcibios_reset_secondary_bus(), which restores a
Root Port on a host bridge that provides reset_root_port().

Skip the save at add when none of those is built and the device is
not on such a root bus.

Under a hypervisor every configuration read is a trap. On this
machine's QEMU q35, eight conventional PCI functions on the root bus
(the host bridge, the LPC bridge and six virtio-pci devices), the save
reads 16 dwords from each: 128 reads per boot, measured with the
function tracer. At about 6.7 us a nested exit, that is ~0.9 ms on the
boot's path.

What it costs: with the save skipped, pci_restore_state() on a device
whose state was never saved writes zeros back over its header. In a
kernel built with none of the error recovery above, that takes a
driver that restores in a path of its own without a pci_save_state()
of its own. Of the drivers this machine's config builds, only 8250_pci
restores, and only in its error handler. A driver that does,
built into a later config, would leave its device unusable after its
own reset, so the list above has to grow with whatever calls error
handlers next.

Measured on the bench fleet (nested KVM, Xeon 6975P-C, one vCPU, the
workspace's shape), alone this patch moved the first SSH login by less
than the noise. With the other four patches that remove exits from
device setup (the IO-APIC table, the state save at add, virtio's
capabilities, queue_select, the MSI message), the cold first login was
9.4 ms faster, 95% interval [5.7, 14.2], over 120 interleaved boots a
side (2026-11-23), and a restored guest's login did not move.

Local patch for this machine. Not upstream.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/pci/bus.c | 23 ++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/bus.c b/drivers/pci/bus.c
index 655ed5343..a8c3794ad 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/bus.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/bus.c
@@ -336,6 +336,26 @@ void __weak pcibios_resource_survey_bus(struct pci_bus *bus) { }
 
 void __weak pcibios_bus_add_device(struct pci_dev *pdev) { }
 
+/*
+ * Whether anything can restore @dev's state without saving it first.
+ * Error recovery can: it calls a driver's error handlers once the error
+ * has lost the state, and those restore what was saved beforehand.
+ * Every other path that loses it, a power transition or a reset,
+ * saves it first. pcie_do_recovery() needs AER, which DPC and EDR
+ * depend on; EEH, s390's zPCI and Xen's PCI frontend call the handlers
+ * themselves. pcibios_reset_secondary_bus() restores a Root Port its
+ * host has reset.
+ */
+static bool pci_dev_needs_saved_state(struct pci_dev *dev)
+{
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_PCIEAER) || IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_EEH) ||
+	    IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_S390) || IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_XEN_PCIDEV_FRONTEND))
+		return true;
+
+	return pci_is_root_bus(dev->bus) &&
+	       pci_find_host_bridge(dev->bus)->reset_root_port;
+}
+
 /**
  * pci_bus_add_device - start driver for a single device
  * @dev: device to add
@@ -358,7 +378,8 @@ void pci_bus_add_device(struct pci_dev *dev)
 	pci_bridge_d3_update(dev);
 
 	/* Save config space for error recoverability */
-	pci_save_state(dev);
+	if (pci_dev_needs_saved_state(dev))
+		pci_save_state(dev);
 
 	/*
 	 * Enable runtime PM, which potentially allows the device to
